<article class="env-proposition" id="0C9X">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C9X">Proposition <span data-tag="0C9X">55.7.4</span>.</a> Let $g \geq 2$. For every numerical type $T$ of genus $g$ and prime number $\ell > 768g$ we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \dim _{\mathbf{F}_\ell } \mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T)[\ell ] \leq g  \]
</div>
  <p> where $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T)$ is as in Definition <a href="/tag/0C7H" data-tag="0C7H">55.4.1</a>. If $T$ is minimal, then we even have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \dim _{\mathbf{F}_\ell } \mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T)[\ell ] \leq g_{top} \leq g  \]
</div>
  <p> where $g_{top}$ as in Definition <a href="/tag/0C79" data-tag="0C79">55.3.11</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Say $T$ is given by $n, m_ i, a_{ij}, w_ i, g_ i$. If $T$ is not minimal, then there exists a $(-1)$-index. After replacing $T$ by an equivalent type we may assume $n$ is a $(-1)$-index. Applying Lemma <a href="/tag/0C7J" data-tag="0C7J">55.4.4</a> we find $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T')$ where $T'$ is a numerical type of genus $g$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/0C77" data-tag="0C77">55.3.9</a>) with $n - 1$ indices. Thus we conclude by induction on $n$ provided we prove the lemma for minimal numerical types. 
    <p>
      Assume that $T$ is a minimal numerical type of genus $\geq 2$. Observe that $g_{top} \leq g$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0C7C" data-tag="0C7C">55.3.14</a>. If $A = (a_{ij})$ then since $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(A)$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0CE7" data-tag="0CE7">55.4.3</a>. Thus it suffices to prove the lemma for $\mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(A)$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0C9W" data-tag="0C9W">55.7.3</a> we see that $m_ i|a_{ij}| \leq 768g$ for all $i, j$. Hence the result by Lemma <a href="/tag/0C6X" data-tag="0C6X">55.2.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>